units: section {
  | The system ships as one Go binary, `heron`, built in four units, one after another; the
  | binary, its wiring and the repository's layout are the [binary](#architecture.runtime.binary)
  | and the [repository layout](#architecture.runtime.layout) components.
  |
  | A **unit** is one of the four parts the binary is built in, each owning its Go packages
  | under `internal/<unit>/`, its standard packages under `std/`, its frontend packages under
  | `web/`, and the fixtures from its tabs.
  |
  | The units are built in order, and each one only adds to those before it, registering
  | its parts through the registry of the first.
  |
  | | Unit                    | Holds                                                                                              | Commands                          | Spec |
  | | ----------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ---- |
  | | 1. Language and runtime | parser, resolver, types, interpreter, lints, formatter, records, generation framework, the registry, the standard packages | `check`, `fmt`, `lint`, `run`, `generate` | [the language](#heron.language) |
  | | 2. Development tools    | language server, editor grammar, prose linting with Vale, docs generator, server and frontend shells, spec browser, code editor and preview, the shared UI | `lsp`, `serve`, `generate docs` | [the tools](#heron.tools) |
  | | 3. DevOps and testing   | test planner, CI adapters, releases, test coordinator, workers, Incus driver, dashboard          | `test`, `worker`, `generate ci`   | not yet specified |
  | | 4. Agents               | prompt processing, harness adapters, agent environments, `delegate`, prompt workbench            | `generate agents`                 | not yet specified |
  |
  | Units 1 and 2 are the [runtime](#architecture.runtime) and the
  | [development tools](#architecture.tools); units 3 and 4 are [deferred](#delivery.roadmap).

  principles: section {
    | How the units relate. The first is checked in CI, as the
    | [binary's invariant](#architecture.runtime.binary.units_import_backwards); the others are kept in review.
    |
    | - **Dependencies point backwards.** No unit imports a later one, and DevOps and testing
    |   and Agents don't import each other; they meet through data: records, the registry and
    |   the server API.
    | - **Seams are registrations.** A unit registers its commands, generation targets,
    |   lints, record kinds, frontend panels, gutter providers and renderers through the
    |   registry, so an earlier unit never knows which later units exist.
  }

  build_order: section {
    | 1. **Language and runtime:** the lossless parser and `heron fmt`; resolution, types
    |    and the core lints; the interpreter, tasks and `heron run`, then the repository's
    |    `setup`; the prose view and the generation framework.
    | 2. **Development tools:** the tree-sitter grammar and `heron lsp`; prose linting
    |    with Vale; the docs renderer and `heron generate docs`; the server and frontend
    |    shells; the code editor with its live preview.
    | 3. **DevOps and testing:** the planner and Incus workers on a local daemon, then
    |    the coordinator with several hosts; CI adapters and releases; the dashboard panel
    |    and gutter results.
    | 4. **Agents:** prompt processing and the harness adapters; `delegate` and agent
    |    environments; the workbench panel and prompt previews.
    |
    | A unit starts only once the one before it meets its own done criteria
    | ([unit 1](#delivery.unit_1), [unit 2](#delivery.unit_2)). The whole build is done only when all four units are,
    | and the binary still builds and passes with each prefix of the units.
  }
}
